Valid.

Confirmed in [DiagnosticReportForm.tsx](src/pages/Facility/services/serviceRequests/components/DiagnosticReportForm.tsx#L340-L352) (this is where the `upsert-observations` gating lives — the review file's own mutation toasts unconditionally):

The batch is built so that `update-report` is **always** pushed, but `upsert-observations` is pushed **only when `formattedObservations.length > 0`** ([lines 712-727](src/pages/Facility/services/serviceRequests/components/DiagnosticReportForm.tsx#L712-L727)):

```ts
if (formattedObservations.length > 0) {
  requests.push({ ...referenceId: "upsert-observations"... });
}
requests.push({ ...referenceId: "update-report"... }); // always
```

And `onSuccess` gates only the toast on that reference, while the side effects run unconditionally ([lines 340-352](src/pages/Facility/services/serviceRequests/components/DiagnosticReportForm.tsx#L340-L352)):

```ts
if (results.some((r) => r.reference_id === "upsert-observations")) {
  toast.success(t("test_results_saved_successfully"));
}
queryClient.invalidateQueries(...);   // always
setIsExpanded(false);                 // always → card collapses
onReportSaved(report.id);             // always
```

So a conclusion-only (or files-only) save produces a batch containing just `update-report`, the toast never fires, yet the card collapses — exactly the silent "did that save?" outcome described.

The reviewer's suggested fix is sound. The cleanest option is a conclusion-specific message in the `else` branch so the wording stays accurate:

```ts
if (results.some((r) => r.reference_id === "upsert-observations")) {
  toast.success(t("test_results_saved_successfully"));
} else {
  toast.success(t("diagnostic_report_saved_successfully"));
}
```

Want me to apply that? It needs a new i18n key `diagnostic_report_saved_successfully` appended to `en.json`.

