content.text:
**`Broken` (legibility)** — this encodes two values into one string as an undocumented protocol between two components.

`onReportSaved` is typed `(reportId: string) => void`, but what is actually passed is `id + ":" + timestamp`. The consumer in `DiagnosticReportReview.tsx` has to decode it:

```
if (expandedReportId?.split(":")[0] === report.id) { setIsExpanded(true); }
```








A reader coming to either file cold cannot tell why the `:`-split exists, and the parameter name (`reportId`) actively says the wrong thing. The timestamp is only there as a nonce so the effect re-fires when the *same* report is saved twice.

Minimal fix — make the shape explicit so no decoding is needed:

```
onReportSaved: (report: { id: string; savedAt: number }) => void;
// ...
onReportSaved({ id: report.id, savedAt: Date.now() });
```








and in the review item:

```
useEffect(() => {
  if (expandedReport?.id === report.id) setIsExpanded(true);
}, [expandedReport, report.id]);
```








Same behaviour, but the nonce is visible as a nonce and the id stays an id.

is this vALID?

